In Islamic art, the avoidance of figural images of the divine pushed artists
toward an extraordinary mastery of pattern. The result is some of the most
intricate geometric ornament ever made, covering walls, domes, and pages in
endless, centered, mathematically exact designs.

## Pattern instead of portrait

Where other traditions placed a deity at the center of a mandala, Islamic
geometric art places pure structure: a star, a rosette, a node from which the
whole pattern radiates and to which the eye returns.

## The infinite implied

The patterns are built so that they could, in principle, extend forever. The
tilework is a finite window onto an unbounded order, a mandala without a face.

This is a placeholder article. The full research piece is in progress.
